  "rationale": "Evidence. Iteration 7 (score 933.8, best) had the seven-sensor array at ±0.04 m, base_speed 0.95, kp 4.5, kd 3.0, and slalom 19/20 finished with 0 line-loss events. Its one slalom timeout is the only remaining failure. Iteration 8 raised base_speed to 1.0 and scored lower (930.8): the oval and s-bend barely sped up, and slalom went back to an off-track exit with steering_oscillation rising to about 0.25 and fewer finishes. So raw speed is not the lever; it costs stability at reversals. Score arithmetic: finished runs already get about 937 from the fixed terms, so the time bonus (60*(1-t/3), about 12 points at 2.37 s) is small. Avoiding a failed slalom run (about 500 points lost) matters far more than speed.\n\nChange (single variable from iteration 7): wheel_track 0.11 -> 0.10 m. Turn rate for a given wheel differential rises as the track narrows, which is the steering authority lever that iteration 4 showed helps the slalom (its narrowing to 0.11 m, together with the lighter body, lifted slalom progress from 0.74 to 0.90). Everything else is held at iteration 7 values: base_speed 0.95, kp 4.5, ki 0, kd 3.0, seven sensors at ±0.04 m (0.0133 m pitch), sensor_size 0.012, weights 50*x, mass 0.6, body 0.15 x 0.12 m, radius 0.03 m, line_loss last_direction.\n\nExpected result: slalom finishes 19 or 20 of 20 with fewer timeouts, mean slalom score at or above 910, and oval and s-bend near 948 and 942. The narrower track may increase yaw response, so steering_oscillation could rise slightly (expected about 0.25 to 0.3).\n\nHypotheses to check next: (1) if steering_oscillation on the slalom exceeds 0.32 or the oval rms rises above 0.008, the narrower track is too twitchy; go back to 0.11 m and raise kd to 3.2. (2) If the slalom still times out at 0.95 with track 0.10, the limit is speed through reversals, so try base_speed 0.975. (3) If all tracks stay at 20/20 finishes and the time falls, a later step can test ki of about 0.1 to remove steady-state bias on the s-bend.\n\nMeasurements requested: all metrics per track, with particular attention to slalom finish count, timeouts versus off-track exits, steering_oscillation, and completion_time on the oval and s-bend.",
